<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Cappadocia</h1><p class="st-meta">331 BCE – 17 CE (approx.) · capital Mazaca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cappadocia, a Hellenistic kingdom of the central Anatolian plateau ruled by Iranian-descended kings who became loyal allies of Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariarathes-i-of-cappadocia">Ariarathes</a>, the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>, defied Alexander's successors until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> killed him in 322 BCE. His adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariarathes-ii-of-cappadocia">Ariarathes II</a> regained the land c. 301 BCE, and his heirs took the title king, ruling the plateau from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Mazaca</a> beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-erciyes">Mount Argaeus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariarathid-dynasty">Ariarathid</a> kings ruled a rural country of large estates and temple domains, such as the shrine of the goddess Ma at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comana-cappadocia">Comana</a>. Iranian traditions mixed with Greek culture; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariarathes-v-of-cappadocia">Ariarathes V</a> was a patron of philosophers. The kingdom supplied horses, slaves and grain and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Pontic interference, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariobarzanes-i-of-cappadocia">Ariobarzanes I</a> in 95 BCE. The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archelaus-of-cappadocia">Archelaus</a>, was summoned to Rome by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and died there in 17 CE, and Cappadocia became a Roman province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Cappadocia" width="320" height="182"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Cappadocia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>331 BCE – 17 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mazaca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ariarathes V, Ariobarzanes I, Archelaus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism, Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Cappadocian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cappadocia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-157&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cappadocia" aria-label="Kingdom of Cappadocia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5561 788 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Cappadocia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5881 1044L6140 1251M5881 1044L6140 1232M5881 1044L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M6140 1232h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5881 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5881 1044h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-157&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cappadocia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
